In this lesson, we'll look at how to factorise monic quadratics. A monic quadratic is any quadratic where the number in front of the x squared term is one, so it looks like x squared plus b x plus c. Factorising just means breaking it into two brackets multiplied together. Here is the simple recipe. You need to find two numbers that multiply to give c and add to give b. Once you spot those two numbers, you can write the quadratic as x plus m times x plus n. A quick tip on signs. If c is positive, your two numbers share the same sign, and if the sum b is positive they are both positive, while if b is negative they are both negative. If c is negative, then your two numbers have opposite signs, and the larger one takes the sign of b. There are also two special patterns to recognise. The difference of two squares, x squared minus a squared, factors as x minus a times x plus a. And a perfect square, x squared plus two a x plus a squared, factors as x plus a all squared. Now let's see it in action.